# Chroma library, modern Earth: NATIONAL STYLE MOMENTS (v25 decision #11, cut item 3; draft 10-23).
# Brief: chroma-ideas/v25-prep/library/briefs/NATIONAL.md. Pours into chroma-library/earth_national-styles.lib (built as
# earth_national.py with the route moments). Source: world_fit.json national.styles and styles_rule.
# Twenty styles (ten pairs, ten triads), two moments each, forty in all. Each moment carries style: (the life's top two or
# three colours) and route: (that style's routes), and turns on the style's tension: the first where the motives pull
# against each other on the route, the second where they combine into something neither gives alone. A style changes
# which dilemma comes up and how the options read; it adds no power or capacity, and every option is open to every colour.
# Shape moments: shape: any, rungs 1 to 4 of the route's five (local, local+, wider, wider+, national).
# Layout: W U B R G once each, then one full cross cycle, alternating by moment:
#   cycle A: W>U U>B B>R R>G G>W       cycle B: W>B U>R B>G R>W G>U
# Fork moments (plateau, cost, chance, round) offer going on, a change of approach, ease (postpone or slow) and stop.

# ================================================================ PAIRS

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W U, Public Systems Reformer: proven procedure versus a better system (pull, cycle A)

== the checklist that never lost a file
tier: life event
shape: any
kind: reaction
rungs: 2 3
fork:
style: W U
route: public_service_architect, systems_inventor
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-80
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, mentor
earth: the longest-serving staff want the old checklist kept, though {Ns} new system halved the waiting in trial
tribal: the oldest keepers of the stores want the old count kept, though {Ns} new tally fed more households last winter
magic: the guild's senior clerks want the old ledgers kept, though {Ns} new reckoning halved the granary queues
scene: In trial at two sites, the system {N} designed cut the waiting by half. The region wants an answer by the end of the month on whether every site switches over, and the longest-serving staff have said it plainly: the old checklist has never lost a file in twenty years, and nobody has yet shown the new one will not.
scene tribal: Last winter {Ns} new tally of the stores fed more households than the old count ever did. Before the moot, the oldest keepers say the old count has never left a family out, and one good winter proves nothing.
scene magic: {Ns} new reckoning halved the queues at the crown's granary in its trial season. The guild's senior clerks want the old ledgers kept, which have not lost a sack in living memory, and the steward wants a decision by the quarter day.
- W | run old and new side by side for a year, then switch on the numbers | v: conformity, security | aim: hold | foot: mark kept your word | chance: 80
- U | publish the trial's figures in full, failures included, and invite the staff to check them | v: self-direction, universalism | aim: hold | foot: lesson open numbers settle arguments | chance: 73
- B | take the figures to the region's head and have the switch ordered from above | v: power, achievement | aim: hold | foot: person boss | chance: 65
- R | switch the next site over next week, and fix what breaks as it breaks | v: stimulation, self-direction | aim: hold | foot: mark took a wild risk | chance: 60
- G | give the old checklist its due, and let the staff keep it at their own sites | v: tradition, benevolence | aim: bend | foot: lesson trust takes longer than proof | chance: 80
- W>U | write each safeguard of the old checklist into the new system, line by line | v: conformity, self-direction | aim: hold | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 72
- U>B | find the one site whose staff want it most, and let its results do the persuading | v: self-direction, achievement | aim: hold | foot: person colleague | chance: 73
- B>R | name a switch date in front of everyone, and dare the doubters to beat the numbers | v: power, stimulation | aim: hold | foot: door made an enemy | chance: 55
- R>G | work a week of shifts beside the oldest hands, and learn what the checklist really guards | v: stimulation, benevolence | aim: bend | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 90
- G>W | shelve the switch a year, and keep the goodwill of those who run it daily | v: security, conformity | aim: bend | foot: lesson a service runs on goodwill | chance: 80
works: By the month's end the argument has moved from whether to how, and {N} can feel the difference.
works: {N} and the staff still disagree on some things, but they are arguing over the same figures now.
fails: The doubts harden on both sides, and the month ends with nothing settled.
fails: Whatever was chosen, a few of the staff go quiet when {N} walks into the room, for a while.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W U: evidence first, then the standard (combine, cycle B)

== a measured pilot becomes the standard
tier: life event
shape: any
kind: practice
rungs: 3 4
fork:
style: W U
route: public_service_architect, systems_inventor
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 24-80
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: joy
life: work, learning
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, mentor
earth: the measured pilot of {Ns} redesign is done, and the work now is writing it into the standard every site keeps
tribal: the new way of keeping the stores has held a winter, and the work now is teaching it so every camp keeps it
magic: the granary trial is done, and the work now is setting it down in the guild's book of standing practice
scene: The pilot is done and measured: fewer missed appointments, shorter waits, the same staff. {N} has six weeks before the board meets to make it the standard every site works to, and a standard that is wrong will be wrong everywhere at once.
scene tribal: The new way of keeping the stores has held a winter at one camp. Before the next moot {N} must teach it so plainly that every camp can keep it, and a mistake taught to all is a mistake in every camp.
scene magic: The trial season at the granary is over, and the guild's masters will set the method in the book of standing practice at the quarter day. Whatever goes into the book is copied in every granary of the realm.
- W | write it in plain steps, and test each at a site that never saw the pilot | v: conformity, security | aim: work | foot: lesson plain steps travel furthest | chance: 75
- U | comb the pilot's data for anything that only worked because of who ran it | v: self-direction, universalism | aim: work | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 68
- B | see each board member alone before the meeting, and know every vote in advance | v: power, achievement | aim: work | foot: person boss | chance: 70
- R | take the draft round the sites in person, and rewrite it on the spot | v: stimulation, self-direction | aim: work | foot: means time-.06 | chance: 75
- G | keep the standard short, and leave each site room for its own ways | v: tradition, benevolence | aim: ease | foot: lesson leave room for local ways | chance: 77
- W>B | build in a monthly check that shows the board the standard is working | v: conformity, achievement | aim: work | foot: door kept your word | chance: 70
- U>R | add one untried improvement, clearly marked as a trial inside the standard | v: self-direction, stimulation | aim: work | foot: mark took a wild risk | chance: 58
- B>G | credit the pilot's staff by name, and have them present it to the board | v: achievement, benevolence | aim: work | foot: person colleague | chance: 80
- R>W | hold open sessions where any site may pick the draft apart, point by point | v: stimulation, conformity | aim: work | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 77
- G>U | let the draft rest a fortnight, then read it again with fresh eyes | v: security, self-direction | aim: ease | foot: means health+.03 | chance: 77
works: The standard goes to the board clear and tested, and the sites that read it recognise their own work in it.
works: Six weeks of plain, careful work, and {N} understands the thing better than before.
fails: The draft keeps growing, and by the meeting it is longer and less clear than the pilot ever was.
fails: A site that was never in the pilot finds a flaw nobody saw, and the weeks go on mending it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W B, Civic Power Builder: duty to all versus loyalty to backers (pull, cycle A)

== the depot the backers want on their side
tier: life event
shape: any
kind: cost
rungs: 2 3 4
fork: pay
style: W B
route: mandate_builder, coalition_builder
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-80
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: weeks
roles: patron, colleague, partner, go-between
earth: the groups that carried {N} this far want the new depot on their side of the river, and the vote is in three weeks
tribal: the families who stood with {N} at the moot want the new well dug on their side of the valley, and the moot is close
magic: the guilds who backed {N} want the new granary built in their quarter, and the council sits within the month
scene: The groups that carried {N} this far want the new bus depot built on their side of the river. The fairer site, by every count of who would use it, is on the other side. The vote is in three weeks, and holding the alliance through it will take every evening {N} has, and some of the household's money for halls and leaflets.
scene tribal: The families who stood with {N} at the last moot want the new well dug at their end of the valley. The households who need it most live at the other end. The moot sits at the next full moon.
scene magic: The guilds who backed {N} on the city council want the new granary in their own quarter. The poor quarter across the river needs it more. The council sits within the month, and the guilds have long memories.
- W | put the fair site to the vote, and spend every evening winning it without the backers | v: conformity, universalism | aim: pay | cost: money .2, time .5, family .3 | foot: mark kept your word | chance: 60
- U | count who would use each site, and carry the backers with the figures | v: self-direction, universalism | aim: pay | cost: money .1, time .4, family .2 | foot: lesson figures can move friends | chance: 68
- B | give the backers their site, and bank the loyalty for a bigger fight | v: power, achievement | aim: pay | cost: money .1, time .2, family .1 | foot: door broke your word | chance: 75
- R | turn the bargain down out loud, and let the backers walk if they will | v: stimulation, self-direction | aim: refuse | foot: mark defied an authority | chance: 60
- G | step back from the alliance's front, and go back to the neighbourhood work | v: benevolence, security | aim: stop | foot: lesson the street work still counts | chance: 80
- W>U | propose a fair rota for where each future project goes, and win the vote on that | v: conformity, self-direction | aim: pay | cost: money .1, time .4, family .2 | foot: door kept your word | chance: 62
- U>B | find a second project the backers want more, and trade that instead | v: self-direction, power | aim: pay | cost: money .2, time .3, family .1 | foot: person go-between | chance: 65
- B>R | vote for the fair site, and dare the backers to break the alliance over it | v: power, stimulation | aim: pay | cost: money .1, time .3, family .2 | foot: door made an enemy | chance: 55
- R>G | spend the three weeks on doorsteps on both banks, hearing people out | v: stimulation, benevolence | aim: pay | cost: money .1, time .5, family .3 | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 70
- G>W | ask to put the vote off a season, so the whole town can talk it over | v: benevolence, conformity | aim: ease | foot: means time+.04 | chance: 72
works: The vote comes and goes, and the alliance is still standing, a little bruised.
works: People who never backed {N} hear how the choice was made, and some of them remember it.
fails: One of the backing groups quietly stops coming to meetings, and the alliance feels thinner.
fails: The weeks cost more at home than anyone expected, and the house is short-tempered for a while.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W B: the institution and the alliance that holds it (combine, cycle B)

== a standing council the alliance could hold
tier: life event
shape: any
kind: chance
rungs: 3 4
fork: take
style: W B
route: mandate_builder, coalition_builder
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-80
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: patron, colleague, mentor, partner
earth: the towns of the valley agree to found a standing council for the region, and {Ns} alliance is asked to write its charter
tribal: the clans of the river agree to a standing moot with its own keepers, and {Ns} kin are asked to set its customs
magic: the free towns of the march agree to found a standing council, and the crown asks {Ns} league to draw its charter
scene: After two bad winters the towns of the valley have agreed to found a standing council for the region, with its own staff and its own budget. They have asked {Ns} alliance to write the charter by spring. A charter written for the alliance would hold until the alliance fell apart; one written for everyone needs the alliance to hold it up for years.
scene tribal: After two hard winters the clans of the river have agreed to a standing moot with keepers of its own. {Ns} kin are asked to set its customs before the thaw, and customs set for one kin last only as long as that kin is strong.
scene magic: The free towns of the march have agreed to a standing council, and the crown has asked {Ns} league to draw its charter by the spring quarter. A charter drawn for the league dies with the league.
- W | take it on, and write a charter that binds the alliance as tightly as anyone else | v: conformity, universalism | aim: take | foot: mark kept your word | chance: 70
- U | ask for a year to study how other regions' councils failed, then write | v: self-direction, security | aim: ease | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 83
- B | take it, and make sure the alliance's people fill the first council's seats | v: power, achievement | aim: take | foot: person patron | chance: 70
- R | draft the whole charter in a week, and put it out before doubts set in | v: stimulation, achievement | aim: take | foot: mark took a wild risk | chance: 60
- G | hand the drafting to the towns' oldest hands, and step out of it with thanks | v: tradition, benevolence | aim: stop | foot: lesson others can carry it | chance: 80
- W>B | write the seats to rotate among the towns, and give the alliance the first turn | v: conformity, power | aim: take | foot: door kept your word | chance: 65
- U>R | write a short trial charter for two years, with a fixed date to rewrite it | v: self-direction, stimulation | aim: try | foot: lesson a trial before a promise | chance: 68
- B>G | bring the groups outside the alliance into the drafting, and share the credit | v: achievement, benevolence | aim: take | foot: person colleague | chance: 67
- R>W | call one open meeting in every town, and write down what each one demands | v: stimulation, conformity | aim: take | foot: means time-.06 | chance: 67
- G>U | decline the charter, and offer the alliance's records to whoever writes it | v: benevolence, universalism | aim: decline | foot: person mentor | chance: 77
works: The council takes shape slowly, and some of what {N} argued for is still in it when it opens.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the alliance comes out of the spring knowing what it is for.
fails: The towns argue over every clause, and the spring deadline slides into summer.
fails: A group left out of the talks makes its anger known, and it takes the season to mend.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W R, People's Champion: the law's way versus the crowd's urgency (pull, cycle A)

== the crowd at the council doors
tier: life event
shape: any
kind: reaction
rungs: 2 3 4
fork:
style: W R
route: mandate_builder, rallying_voice
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-80
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: trouble
life: community, work
horizon: week
roles: patron, friend, colleague, boss
earth: hundreds wait outside the council doors over the closing clinic, and the agenda says the matter is next month
tribal: the young of three camps crowd the moot's edge over the fouled spring, and the elders say it is not yet their turn to speak
magic: the commons fill the square below the council over the closing almshouse, and the clerk says the matter waits for the next sitting
scene: The clinic on the east side closes at the end of the month, and hundreds of people are outside the council doors tonight. Inside, the agenda says the matter will be heard properly next month, after the clinic has gone. The people outside want {N} to stand up now, break the order of business and force a vote tonight.
scene tribal: The spring above three camps has gone foul, and their young crowd the edge of the moot with their water jars. The elders say the matter comes in its turn, after the grazing; the young want {N} to stand and speak before it.
scene magic: The almshouse closes at the month's end, and the commons fill the square below the council chamber. The clerk says the matter waits for the next sitting. The crowd want {N} to break the order of the day.
- W | keep the order of business, and hold the council to a date before the closure | v: conformity, security | aim: bend | foot: mark kept your word | chance: 75
- U | read the council's rules again for any lawful way to bring the matter forward tonight | v: self-direction, achievement | aim: hold | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 65
- B | trade the crowd's patience for a private promise from the chair | v: power, security | aim: bend | foot: person boss | chance: 67
- R | stand up, break the order of business, and demand the vote tonight | v: stimulation, universalism | aim: hold | foot: mark defied an authority | chance: 55
- G | go outside, stand with the crowd, and let the meeting run without {N} | v: benevolence, universalism | aim: hold | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 67
- W>U | move a formal motion to hear the clinic first, and argue it within the rules | v: conformity, self-direction | aim: hold | foot: lesson rules have doors in them | chance: 62
- U>B | count the votes inside before moving, and only move if they are there | v: self-direction, power | aim: hold | foot: person colleague | chance: 63
- B>R | tell the chair the crowd will stay all night unless the clinic is heard | v: power, stimulation | aim: hold | foot: door made an enemy | chance: 55
- R>G | bring three of the clinic's patients inside to speak in the public seats | v: stimulation, benevolence | aim: hold | foot: mark helped someone in need | chance: 65
- G>W | ask the crowd to go home tonight, and promise to bring them back for the hearing | v: benevolence, conformity | aim: bend | foot: door kept your word | chance: 72
works: Something moves: a date, a vote or a promise, and the crowd goes home with more than it came with.
works: Inside and outside, people see where {N} stood tonight, and nobody is left guessing.
fails: The night ends with nothing settled, and some of the crowd are angry with {N} on the way out.
fails: The council grumbles for weeks about how the evening went, and {N} has to mend it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W R: the street's demand taken into the hall (combine, cycle B)

== the march's demands as a motion
tier: life event
shape: any
kind: practice
rungs: 2 3 4
fork:
style: W R
route: mandate_builder, rallying_voice
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-80
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: joy
life: community, work
horizon: months
roles: friend, colleague, mentor, patron
earth: the march filled the streets, and now its six demands have to become a motion the council can actually vote on
tribal: the young carried their jars to the moot, and now their anger has to become a proposal the elders can accept
magic: the commons filled the square, and now their cry has to become a petition the council is bound to answer
scene: The march filled the streets last month, and the council has agreed to hear its case at the next sitting, in eight weeks. The six demands on the banners were written for a crowd. A motion has to be costed, worded and carried, and the people who marched will judge {N} on whether it still sounds like them.
scene tribal: The young of three camps carried their water jars to the moot, and the elders have agreed to hear them at the next moon. Their anger was loud and true; a proposal the elders can accept has to say who digs and who pays.
scene magic: The commons filled the square, and the council is bound by old custom to answer a petition put to it properly. Their cry was written on walls; a petition must be argued clause by clause.
- W | turn each demand into a clause the council can vote on, one at a time | v: conformity, universalism | aim: work | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 72
- U | cost each demand properly, and drop the one that cannot be paid for | v: self-direction, universalism | aim: work | foot: lesson a costed demand gets heard | chance: 68
- B | find the two council members who could carry it, and draft it with them | v: power, achievement | aim: work | foot: person colleague | chance: 67
- R | write it in the marchers' own words, and dare the council to vote it down | v: stimulation, universalism | aim: work | foot: mark defied an authority | chance: 60
- G | keep the march's people meeting every week, and let the motion wait for them | v: benevolence, tradition | aim: ease | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 77
- W>B | draft it by the council's own rules, and sign up allies on every side | v: conformity, power | aim: work | foot: door kept your word | chance: 65
- U>R | test the draft on a hall full of marchers, and rewrite what they boo | v: self-direction, stimulation | aim: work | foot: means time-.05 | chance: 65
- B>G | let the people most hurt by the problem present the motion themselves | v: achievement, benevolence | aim: work | foot: mark helped someone in need | chance: 70
- R>W | keep the pressure on outside while the motion is argued inside | v: stimulation, conformity | aim: work | foot: person friend | chance: 65
- G>U | slow the drafting down, and ask the oldest organisers what failed last time | v: tradition, self-direction | aim: ease | foot: person mentor | chance: 74
works: The motion goes in sounding like the people who marched, and the council has to take it seriously.
works: Weeks of drafting at kitchen tables, and {N} comes out of it knowing how a demand becomes a decision.
fails: The motion comes out careful and grey, and some of the marchers say it is not theirs any more.
fails: The drafting drags, the energy of the march drains away, and the hall is half empty at the hearing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W G, Commons Steward: fair rules versus what the place needs (pull, cycle A)

== equal days on a tired river
tier: life event
shape: any
kind: plateau
rungs: 2 3
fork: go_on
style: W G
route: common_ground_maker, living_land_steward
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-82
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: mixed
life: nature, community
horizon: years
roles: neighbour, elder, colleague, rival
earth: the settlement {N} brokered gives every user equal days on the river, and after four years the fish have not come back
tribal: the custom {N} set gives every camp equal days at the fishing pools, and four summers on the pools are still poor
magic: the charter {N} drew gives every guild equal days on the river, and four years on the fish are still thin
scene: Four years ago {N} brokered the settlement that ended the quarrel over the river: every user, from the anglers to the farm that draws water, gets equal days. It has held, and nobody quarrels now. But the fish counts have not moved in four years, the water is low again this summer, and the users meet next month to renew the settlement as it stands.
scene tribal: Four summers ago {N} set the custom that ended the feud over the fishing pools: every camp has its equal days. The feud has not come back, and neither have the fish. The camps meet at midsummer to renew the custom.
scene magic: Four years ago {N} drew the charter that gave every guild equal days on the river, and the guilds have kept it to the letter. The fish are still thin, the water is low, and the charter comes up for renewal at the summer quarter.
- W | renew the settlement exactly as it stands, because it is what keeps the peace | v: conformity, security | aim: work | foot: mark kept your word | chance: 80
- U | put four years of fish and water counts before every user ahead of the meeting | v: self-direction, universalism | aim: work | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 71
- B | press the biggest users to give up days first, and make it a condition of renewal | v: power, universalism | aim: work | foot: door made an enemy | chance: 60
- R | propose closing the river entirely for one summer, and argue it at the meeting | v: stimulation, universalism | aim: work | foot: mark took a wild risk | chance: 55
- G | hand the settlement to the users' own keepers, and step away from the table | v: benevolence, tradition | aim: stop | foot: lesson the peace was worth making | chance: 80
- W>U | keep equal days, but let the counts decide each year how many days there are | v: conformity, self-direction | aim: work | foot: lesson a fair rule can learn | chance: 70
- U>B | ask the farm to fund a weir that keeps the water up, for its days | v: self-direction, power | aim: work | foot: person neighbour | chance: 61
- B>R | let the users quarrel openly again for a season, if that is what it takes | v: power, stimulation | aim: ease | foot: means ties-.04 | chance: 65
- R>G | walk the whole river with the oldest anglers, and hear what they remember | v: stimulation, tradition | aim: work | foot: person elder | chance: 80
- G>W | renew it for one more year as it is, and ask for time to think | v: security, conformity | aim: ease | foot: means time+.04 | chance: 77
works: The meeting is long and slow, but it ends with the users talking about the river and not only about their days.
works: Whatever was decided, {N} comes away knowing more about what the settlement does and does not do.
fails: The users renew it out of habit, and nobody says aloud what everyone can see in the water.
fails: Old quarrels stir again at the meeting, and it takes the summer to settle them.

